From e722468e9398e7195381245e011fe94332997076 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shammah Chancellor <shammah.chancellor@notvalid.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 13:39:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] test: write mocked peers.dat with the active chain's disk
 magic

feature_addrman builds its own peers.dat and hard-coded the upstream
regtest disk magic, fabfb5da. v2/061 gave Lotus V2 its own network
identity, including regtest disk magic 8d99bfcc, which addrdb checks
when it reads peers.dat. The node therefore refused the very first
mocked file with "Invalid network magic number", and the test stopped
before checking anything else.

The fixture now takes the magic from DISK_MAGIC_BYTES in the test
framework, which each tier already keeps in step with its chain
parameters (the eCash tier still lists fabfb5da), so the test is
correct on both tiers. The deliberately wrong-magic case still uses
deadbeef.

Tier: V2 (fixup to v2/061)
---
 test/functional/feature_addrman.py | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/test/functional/feature_addrman.py b/test/functional/feature_addrman.py
index db78b64ebb..1c62ff954e 100644
--- a/test/functional/feature_addrman.py
+++ b/test/functional/feature_addrman.py
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@
 import os
 
 from test_framework.messages import hash256, ser_uint256
+from test_framework.p2p import DISK_MAGIC_BYTES
 from test_framework.test_framework import BitcoinTestFramework
 from test_framework.test_node import ErrorMatch
 from test_framework.util import assert_equal
@@ -15,7 +16,7 @@ def serialize_addrman(
     *,
     addrman_format=1,
     lowest_compatible=4,
-    net_magic=b"\xfa\xbf\xb5\xda",
+    net_magic=DISK_MAGIC_BYTES["regtest"],
     bucket_key=1,
     len_new=None,
     len_tried=None,
